The difficulty estimates (scripts/calibre/jiten/) include material from Jiten
(https://github.com/Sirush/Jiten), licensed under the Apache License 2.0 (see
scripts/calibre/jiten/LICENSE):
- deconjugator.json, Jiten's deconjugation rules;
- features.py and deconjugator.py, a Python port of Jiten's feature extraction;
- chimi-difficulty*.onnx, models trained on Jiten's features and
  on difficulty scores published by jiten.moe.
These files were modified or derived for this project. Jiten is not affiliated
with this project.

scripts/calibre/jiten/data/jiten_freq_global.zip is Jiten's word frequency
list from jiten.moe (https://jiten.moe/frequency-dictionaries), by Jiten,
licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/),
redistributed unmodified; files packed from it (frequency.ygp) are under the
same licence. See scripts/calibre/jiten/data/README.md.

scripts/calibre/jiten/kanji-grades.json is derived from KANJIDIC2 by the
Electronic Dictionary Research and Development Group
(https://www.edrdg.org/kanjidic/), licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0; it is
distributed under the same licence.
scripts/calibre/jiten/goshu.json.gz holds word origins (語種) from UniDic
(https://clrd.ninjal.ac.jp/unidic/, via unidic-lite), Copyright (c) 2011-2013,
The UniDic Consortium, used under its BSD licence.
The readings in context and the "Known" furigana check are yurigana
(https://github.com/blankiekun/yurigana), BSD 3-Clause, a dependency installed
from its release; its kanji-readings.json is derived from KANJIDIC2 (CC BY-SA
4.0) and its reading model is trained on public-domain works from Aozora Bunko
(https://www.aozora.gr.jp/) and on sentences from Tatoeba
(https://tatoeba.org/), CC BY 2.0 FR. See yurigana's NOTICE. Its
jmdict-pairs.json.gz, from JMdict (EDRDG, CC BY-SA 4.0, below), also gives the
word lists the compounds they join (scripts/calibre/jiten/compound_merge.py),
kept in the frequency pack the server and the analysis tools build.

The built-in dictionary's one-tap download (dictionaries/JMdict_english.zip in
the built site, fetched from jmdict-yomitan's latest release when the Pages
site and the web image are built; it isn't in the repository) is JMdict
(https://www.edrdg.org/wiki/index.php/JMdict-EDICT_Dictionary_Project) by the
Electronic Dictionary Research and Development Group, licensed under CC BY-SA
4.0 (https://www.edrdg.org/edrdg/licence.html), in the Yomitan format made by
jmdict-yomitan (https://github.com/yomidevs/jmdict-yomitan), redistributed
unmodified.

The visual novel player (apps/web/static/vn/conscripter.js and conscripter.wasm) is
CONscripter (https://github.com/blankiekun/CONscripter), a fork of
ONScripter-yuri (https://github.com/YuriSizuku/OnscripterYuri), based on
ONScripter-Jh and ONScripter by Ogapee, licensed under the GNU General Public
License version 2. It is the web build of the CONscripter release named in
apps/web/static/vn/conscripter-version.txt, fetched by scripts/conscripter/build.sh;
that release's tag in the repository above is its complete corresponding
source. Its fallback font
(apps/web/static/vn/default.ttf) is Noto Sans JP, licensed under the SIL Open
Font License 1.1.

The desktop app (apps/desktop) bundles Yomitan
(https://github.com/yomidevs/yomitan), a separate program licensed under the
GNU General Public License version 3, unmodified: the Chrome build of the
release named in apps/desktop/scripts/fetch-yomitan.sh, fetched at build time
with its licence text. That release's tag is its complete corresponding source.

